 :adr/body "Asked to continue the cljs -> Kotoba refactor from kotoba-lang/kotoba-script#100,
make it deployable on Cloudflare, and give reagent and re-frame Kotoba
equivalents so ClojureScript does not have to change much.

## The decision

`kotoba-lang/shitsuke` gains the re-frame pair as a Kotoba library --
`kotoba/reframe_core.kotoba` (db, event, subscription, effect) and
`kotoba/reagent_core.kotoba` (element builders; a view is a `:document`) --
plus a portable bridge (`shitsuke.kotoba.guest`), two additive seam entries
(`rf/install-guest!`, `r/kotoba-view`) and a Cloudflare Worker host
(`runtime/reframe-worker.mjs`).

The point is the CALL SITES. `(rf/dispatch [:counter/inc])` and
`@(rf/subscribe [:counter/count])` are unchanged, and nothing at the call site
says the handler now runs in Kotoba. A component moves one event id at a time.

## Why the split falls where it does

re-frame's own signatures are already what a Kotoba function can be:
`db + event -> db` and `db + query -> value`. Three things stay on the host
side, and none of them is a gap:

  * the REGISTRY. `reg-event-db` installs a handler at runtime; Kotoba
    dispatch is static, so an app module spells its registry as one `cond`
    over `event-id` -- the same closed dispatch `defmulti`/`defmethod`
    desugars to (guest-grammar `:closed-multimethod`).
  * `app-db` ITSELF. `atom` in Kotoba is `:atom-local` (lang/local-state.edn):
    a let-bound cell that may not be returned, stored or captured. So the db
    is a value in and a value out, which is what re-frame's handler signature
    always said, and what lets SSR and the browser share one handler.
  * PERFORMING EFFECTS. A handler returns an inert `{:db .. :fx [[id v] ..]}`.
    Both guest modules compile with an EMPTY `requiredCapabilities` and are
    granted nothing, even for an app that sends mail.

## The boundary is EDN text, and that is what makes it a seam

The guest prints and reads its own values (`document-edn-print` /
`document-edn-read`), so the crossing is `pr-str` one way and `read-string`
the other, and what app code holds is ordinary Clojure data.

Measured 2026-09-09 through the emitted ESM: the guest's EDN reader accepts
`pr-str` output as it comes -- commas, any key order, keys the handler does
not know, escaped quotes, non-ASCII. Had it wanted canonical bytes, every call
site would have had to learn a codec, and the claim in the request -- that
cljs does not change much -- would have been false.

Not JSON, deliberately: JSON has no keyword and no i64, and the restricted-ESM
ABI hands an i64 across as a BigInt, which `JSON.stringify` refuses outright.

`document-print` / `document-read` remain available as the canonical codec.
Measured the same day: it is HEX, and malformed input traps with a named code
(`document-read-hex-charset`, `-truncated`, `-odd`), which is what lets a host
answer 400 instead of guessing.

## Cloudflare

A restricted-ESM artifact from `kotoba -M compile --target js` RUNS ON WORKERD.
Measured 2026-09-09, wrangler 4.103.0 `wrangler dev --local`: amu's
`examples/todo-app.kotoba` answered a request in 17 ms, and the acceptance for
this ADR's own Worker host passes 10/10 checks -- an i64 crossing
`POST /api/step` and coming back, ordered effects out of `POST /api/dispatch`,
and 400/404/405 on the three refusal paths.

This is a second route to Cloudflare, not the first: `gftdcojp/ai-gftd-dougaka`
already serves a wasm32-browser guest from a Worker (measured 2026-08-31). The
ESM route needs no `WebAssembly.Module` import and no bundler step.

`runtime/reframe-worker.mjs` is 135 lines of mechanism. Every judgement is the
guest's; the one judgement the host makes is that a guest trap on
caller-supplied text is a 400.

## What was measured, and what each check would have missed

  kotoba -M test         39 assertions over :jvm-kir, :js and :wasm
                         (reagent_core 15, reframe_core 24)
  guest_acceptance.cljs  14 checks against the EMITTED ESM, nbb, JVM-free
  reframe_worker_...cljs 10 checks against workerd over HTTP
  clojure -M:test        91 tests / 1158 assertions, the whole shitsuke suite

Each of the four was broken on purpose once and the RIGHT check went red:
`child` stopped appending (2 of 15 red, all three targets), `fx-effect`
stopped building a pair list (fx tests only), `document->hiccup` dropped
children (1 of 14), the Worker answered 500 instead of 400 (1 of 10), and
`perform-fx!` stopped throwing on an effect nobody performs (1 of 18).

Two failures were found this way rather than by reading:

  * `children-of` defaulted to `document-null`, so counting the children of a
    LEAF failed `document-container-required` on all three targets. The module
    compiled; only running it said so.
  * `db-has?` compared `document-contains` to 1. That head is typed `:bool`,
    unlike `string-contains?` which is `:i64` -- the `?` is in one name and the
    type is in neither.

## Bounds, named rather than hidden

  * a `:document` holds 256 nodes -- today's ceiling on one Kotoba screen.
  * attribute values are strings in `reagent_core`.
  * an instance's fuel is spent and never replenished, so the host makes a
    fresh instance per call. Sound only because the guest is pure.
  * a Kotoba app module is a multi-file project: `module-lock` then
    `compile --module-lock`, not the single-file path.
  * the kotoba CLI needs ABSOLUTE paths. A relative input is
    \"input must be a regular file\"; a relative --output reports `:ok true` and
    writes into the compiler's own working directory, not the caller's.

## What this is NOT

It is not a migrated production component. `kotoba/example_counter.kotoba` is
the consumer that proves the libraries are usable as libraries -- an index hit
is not evidence that something works, and neither is a library with no caller.
Moving a real cljs app is the next step and is a whole-component build under
`lang/q9-migration.edn`, whose wave 2 through 5 are `:not-authorized`.

It is also not a production deploy. The Worker evidence is workerd via
`wrangler dev --local`; nothing was deployed.

The browser half is unexercised here: `install-guest!`'s `:cljs` branch needs
real re-frame, which needs a shadow-cljs build, which needs a JVM. The seam
test covers the `:clj` branch (mini runtime) and the bridge acceptance covers
the artifact; a browser build covers neither and remains a gap.
